remstart
    THE LONG VISIT, AND ITS PUZZLES

    this is a prototype of the game that is left when the player stays down there with Ava. the board comes
    back, but none of the numbers do: no moves, no multiplier, no bar of the round, no rounds, no score.
    there is a list of boards of fish that do not come back when they pop, and the goal is an empty sea. she
    says a few words as a board comes in, and one more thing when it is clear. so the talk goes as far as the
    player has got through the boards, and not by any clock.

    the way in is still the FISH_PUZZLE environment variable, because the goodbye that offers it is not built, or the
    Ava window of FISH_DEBUG, which puts her and the game in any state, from the main menu or from a game
    (frame_debug_ava). a player who has gone in has stayed, and the game remembers it (PUZZLE_PREF + 2, puzzleStaying)
    with or without the variable. the main menu then has Resume, which goes straight back to the board that the
    player got to, and New Game, which asks first (see LEAVING). a number in FISH_PUZZLE only says where to start when
    there is no progress yet. FISH_PUZZLE_BOARD, from 1, jumps to a board no matter what. the variables only get the
    player in: once a usual game has started, or the Ava window has been used, they do nothing (PUZZLE_ENV_SPENT).

    IT IS DEEP DOWN HERE
    the sea is dark, the way that it is at night, but it is not night. PUZZLE_DEEP# only goes to what is drawn (see
    stepTide). nothing else looks at it, so the fish are awake, and there are no night sounds and no jellyfish.

    THE MENUS DOWN HERE
    there is nothing to look up and nothing is carried, so the pause menu has no Inventory and no Codec, and a new game
    is a thing for the main menu, where she asks about it.

    LEAVING
    New Game goes down into the empty sea like any other, and Ava comes up in the usual scene and asks if the
    player is leaving, before a board is dealt (puzzle_ask). staying carries on from the same board. leaving
    forgets that the player stayed, and a new game of the usual kind starts from there (PUZZLE_LEFT, and
    menu_start_game). the boards keep their place, because she says that she will keep it. what comes after she
    is gone is not built.

    the game remembers that the player left (PUZZLE_GONE_PREF, puzzleGone). she does not come back: a new game is never
    a puzzle, and there is no first dive, because she is the one who gave it. "Include intro" gets a few big words in
    the empty water instead (puzzle_gone_intro). what else changes when she has gone is not built.

    WHERE SHE IS
    while the player works, she wanders around the sea like any other fish (frame_puzzle_swim). she keeps out
    from behind the fish: she goes across the board in a lane of open water, over the highest fish or
    under the sea floor (puzzleLaneY), and she only changes lanes out past the side of the board. she can be a
    little in front of everything or a little behind it, bigger and brighter in front, and smaller and
    dimmer behind, and she changes that out past the side of the board too.

    there is no box while she does. a line comes apart into its words (PUZZLE_WORDS), and they sit in rows over her
    head. nothing pushes a word around, and there are no physics: before the first word comes out, every word is
    given a row and a place along it (puzzleWordsLayOut). her voice sets the pace: a word comes out when she gets to it.
    it pops out of her mouth, small, and flies up to its place (puzzleWordOut), and it stays there. her swimming has
    nothing to do with that. when the player does something she says something about it right away, so she does not go
    anywhere first: she turns to the right, where she is, and the paragraph is laid out over her.

    a row ends at the end of a phrase, if it has got long enough. when one is finished it moves up a row. under the
    fish there is only room for one row, so it fades, and her voice waits for it (puzzleRowBeforeIsUp).

    she finishes a thought. if there is something new to say while she is saying a line, the rest of that line goes
    at twice the speed (PUZZLE_SAY_PUSHED), and the new thing comes right after it. the words of the old line go at
    once, in PUZZLE_SAY_GONE_MS, and the new one waits for them, so that two lines are never on top of each other.

    the rows that she has finished drift up, slowly and all together (PUZZLE_SAY_RISE#). once the line is out,
    the whole paragraph keeps going until its lowest word is out of the fish, and then it stops, and fades when
    its turn comes. under the fish there is only room for one row, and no way up that is not through them, so
    there a row stays where it is and fades, before she swims back for the next one (puzzleWordsGo). the confirm
    button lets out the rest of a line at once, each word in its place.

    it is the guide all the same: guideSay and guideThen work as they always do, and the line itself is still
    in ANGLER_SAY_TEXT_ID, out of sight, because that is what her voice follows. only where the words are drawn is
    different.

    a talk is another thing. she swims down to where her picture goes, and the usual scene comes up around
    her. it goes away again and she swims off. a puzzle is nine across and seven down, and it sits higher
    than a game does, so that the box of the scene does not cover the sea floor (PUZZLE_FLOOR_Y).

    THE TALKS
    every third board has a talk after it, and that is the usual scene, the same one as asking her a
    question: the dark comes down, she swims in, and the replies are the usual list. she says two things,
    the player picks one of two replies, she answers that, and she says one more thing (puzzle_talk).
    the words are in build/make_ava_talk.py, which makes fish_puzzle_talk. there are more of them than there are
    places for, so PUZZLE_TALK_PICK says which ones are used. each one that needs another comes after it: Gary before
    what happened to Gary, the moon that she saves before the moon that she asks for. the first boards have words of
    their own, and the rest have a remark that goes by how far along the player is (puzzleRemark). the board that a new
    fish comes in on has her saying what it does (puzzleOpening).

    UNDO
    the first special move key takes back the last swap, whatever it popped. puzzleUndoPush keeps the board
    as a swap starts, and PUZZLE_UNDO_MAX of them are kept. a new board forgets them. a puzzle has no moves
    of its own on that key, so nothing else wants it. the key counts while the fish are still popping and
    falling, and it happens the moment that they stop, so that it never feels ignored.

    WHY THERE ARE PUZZLES
    she is afraid that nobody stays without a reason, so she made them one. the boards are a bribe, and
    they run out. she says so, and that the player can go, and then there is no board, no goal and nothing
    to do (PUZZLE_ALONE_MS). this is saved, so a player who closes the game and comes back gets the same
    thing, sooner. the player who stays anyway gets the first thing that she ever said at the
    upgrade screen, "You're still here?", and that is the happy ending. nothing else happens.

    then she says that she made another one, and that it is bad, and the boards go on, just because (PUZZLE_AFTER).
    those will be made as they are needed. for now it is the same list again, as a stand-in.

    THE RULES
    the fish pop the way that they always do: every group of 3 or more that touch, and then the fish over
    them fall, and that can pop more. nothing comes in from the top. any number of swaps can be made, and
    a swap that pops nothing costs nothing. a fish cannot be swapped into an empty cell. the cursor can
    go over one. so the fish in a column that has been emptied can never go past it, and a board that has
    been cut in two stays cut in two, which is what the puzzle is about. the swaps wait for the board to
    settle, which keeps the game on the rules that the puzzles were proven with.

    no new rainbow fish or bubbles are made, and there is no coral, no schools and no tide, because any of those can
    leave a fish behind that the proof of the board did not count on. each is switched off where it starts,
    by asking PUZZLE_ON. the ones that a board starts with are another matter. see THE FISH.

    THE FISH
    the board plays by the rules of the game. a swap has to pop something, or it is not made (puzzleBlocksSwap). that
    goes for every board. three fish change what can be done, and all three do just what they do in a game:
        belly up  it pops when the cursor comes to it, with any that touch it (popBellyUpFish). so it is a pop that needs
                  no group, and the cursor has to go around one to keep it.
        bubbled   its swaps are free, so it is the only fish that moves without popping. the board is looked at after
                  each one, so what it puts together pops, and every step pushes a fish one cell over and leaves it
                  there. a walk that is not thought through pops the wrong group, or leaves fish that can never meet.
                  that is why free swaps are not the way out that they sound like. two bubbles that touch both pop,
                  whatever kinds they are.
        rainbow   three that touch all pop, whatever kinds they are (rainbowWildAhead), and they match with their own
                  kind until then. they do not make more (rainbowBreedGroup), and there are no bursts.

    UNDO AND START OVER CUT THROUGH EVERYTHING
    the player can see that they goofed before the fish have finished popping, so these do not wait for the board to
    settle. a swap only changes the board once it has played out, and what undo kept comes from before it started,
    so nothing has to be finished first: the combo stops where it is (puzzleWantsBack, _combos_stopped), and
    puzzle_stop_move clears up what it left going. every fish is then put back, not only the ones that changed,
    because a fish can be halfway through a fall (PUZZLE_UNDO_ALL).

    HINTS
    with FISH_DEBUG, the window has a hint button. there is no solver in the game: build/make_puzzle_boards.py finds every
    position of every board, a bubble and all, and writes down the ones that can still win, with the move from each that
    is nearest to the end (puzzleHintRow). a position is found by a key made from its fish and from the part of the sea
    that the cursor can get to (puzzleHintKey). the two fish of the swap hop until a move is made. a position that is
    not there is lost (puzzleIsLost), and it is looked for in what undo has kept, which says how many swaps to take back.

    A BOARD IS NEVER A GUESS
    fish_puzzles is made by build/make_puzzle_boards.py, which builds every board backwards from an empty sea, and then
    solves it all the way. the first ten are only swaps. then belly up fish come in, then bubbles, then rainbow fish, and
    they are there to make the later boards harder, so there are still boards without them, and a board does not have to
    need its fish. only the first of each kind does. going around a belly up fish costs nothing, so every other board
    with one has to lose at least a fifth of the time that one is popped, or it is only a free pop. some have a gate:
    belly up fish on a diagonal, which do not touch and which the cursor cannot get between, closing off a corner, or
    two diagonals that stand on the floor like a tent. long_puzzles_all_clear, in fish_tests_puzzle, plays the
    line of each board through the real game, and at every step of it long_puzzles_agree_with_the_game makes a few other
    moves as well, and looks at where they end up. a change to what a pop does has to pass those.

    the info key puts the board back at any time. there is no cost to that.

    THE SNOW STOPS
    she does not say when a swap has lost the board. being told at once was no fun. the water says it, and only if the
    player looks: the snow slows down, over a few seconds, until it hangs still, and it starts again when the swap is
    taken back (puzzleLostLook, stepPuzzleStill). she only speaks once the snow has stopped and there is not a move left
    to make, because then the player has to be told about undo.

    the little boards of the tutorial already know how to have gaps, so a puzzle is one of those:
    TUTORIAL_BOARD is 1 while one is up, and TUTORIAL_STAGE stays at 0.
remend

